{{also|Lucifer|Lúcifer|lucífer}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Originally a [[brand name]] for matches made by Samuel Jones from 1830, soon used [[generic]]ally for self-igniting matches of any brand. From {{der|en|la|lucifer||bringer of light}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-lucifer.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|lucifers|luciferi}}

# {{senseid|en|strike-anywhere match}} {{lb|en|British|archaic}} A self-igniting [[match]], i.e. one which could be [[light|lit]] by [[strike|striking]] on any [[surface]] (as opposed to [[safety match]]es which only light against the material on the side of the [[box]]).
#: {{syn|en|barnburner<id:strike-anywhere match><qq:Mid-Atlantic US>}}
#: {{ant|en|safety match}}
#* '''1915''', {{w|George Asaf}}, song ''Pack up your Troubles''
#*: While you’ve a lucifer to light your fag,<br>Smile, boys, that’s the style.

